WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.445
Lesson 11.5.

00:00:02.445 --> 00:00:05.369
Incident response: the first 60 minutes.

00:00:05.369 --> 00:00:07.644
By the end of this lesson, you'll be

00:00:07.644 --> 00:00:09.919
able to know exactly what to do when

00:00:09.919 --> 00:00:12.747
a protocol you use is exploited, a stablecoin

00:00:12.747 --> 00:00:15.084
depegs, or your wallet is compromised.

00:00:16.384 --> 00:00:17.674
Here's the plan.

00:00:17.674 --> 00:00:19.359
First, the key ideas.

00:00:19.359 --> 00:00:22.074
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:22.074 --> 00:00:23.599
Then, your checklist.

00:00:23.599 --> 00:00:25.761
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:27.061 --> 00:00:29.541
0–5 min: Verify.

00:00:29.541 --> 00:00:35.531
Official channels only (bookmarked X/Discord/status page, security firms).

00:00:35.531 --> 00:00:37.288
Don't click links in DMs, and

00:00:37.288 --> 00:00:39.871
don't sign anything "to protect funds".

00:00:39.871 --> 00:00:42.156
5–20 min: Contain.

00:00:42.156 --> 00:00:44.746
20–60 min: Stabilise.

00:00:44.746 --> 00:00:47.558
Check HFs across all positions (prices may

00:00:47.558 --> 00:00:49.978
be moving), top up from the reserve,

00:00:49.978 --> 00:00:52.960
and journal every action with timestamps.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:54.335
After: Review.

00:00:54.335 --> 00:00:56.697
What was the signal, what worked, and what

00:00:56.697 --> 00:00:59.150
changes in the caps and the register?

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:02.633
First, Protocol exploit: withdraw if still possible

00:01:02.633 --> 00:01:06.215
and safe; revoke approvals to affected contracts.

00:01:06.215 --> 00:01:10.092
Next, Depeg: follow your written peg rule (e.g.

00:01:10.092 --> 00:01:13.888
exit below 0.99 for X hours); don't panic-sell

00:01:13.888 --> 00:01:16.450
far below it on a thin book.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:20.350
And finally, Wallet compromise: move remaining assets to

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:23.635
a fresh wallet (new seed, clean device); revoke

00:01:23.635 --> 00:01:26.440
approvals; assume the old seed is burned.

00:01:27.741 --> 00:01:30.256
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:30.256 --> 00:01:32.724
A lending protocol you use announces

00:01:32.724 --> 00:01:35.226
a paused market after an exploit.

00:01:35.226 --> 00:01:38.053
You verify on its bookmarked status page,

00:01:38.053 --> 00:01:41.419
see withdrawals still work on unaffected markets,

00:01:41.419 --> 00:01:44.515
withdraw your USDC, revoke the pool approval,

00:01:44.515 --> 00:01:47.306
check your other HFs, and journal it.

00:01:47.306 --> 00:01:49.636
Total time: 25 minutes.

00:01:49.636 --> 00:01:51.051
Loss: none.

00:01:51.051 --> 00:01:52.976
Because the steps were written in advance.

00:01:54.276 --> 00:01:55.736
Here's your checklist.

00:01:55.736 --> 00:01:57.271
Do it now, for real.

00:01:57.271 --> 00:02:00.766
First, Incident playbook printed/saved offline.

00:02:00.766 --> 00:02:04.848
Next, Official channels bookmarked for every protocol I use.

00:02:04.848 --> 00:02:08.063
And finally, A clean "fresh wallet" procedure written.

00:02:09.363 --> 00:02:10.573
Question 1.

00:02:10.573 --> 00:02:12.268
First thing to do in an incident?

00:02:16.688 --> 00:02:19.948
The answer: Verify through official, bookmarked channels.

00:02:21.248 --> 00:02:22.493
Question 2.

00:02:22.493 --> 00:02:25.188
Someone offers a link to "rescue your funds".

00:02:25.188 --> 00:02:26.038
What do you do?

00:02:30.458 --> 00:02:32.273
The answer: Ignore it.

00:02:32.273 --> 00:02:34.223
It's a common scam during incidents.

00:02:35.523 --> 00:02:36.793
Question 3.

00:02:36.793 --> 00:02:39.528
Wallet compromised: can you keep using the seed?

00:02:43.948 --> 00:02:46.616
The answer: No. Move to a fresh wallet with

00:02:46.616 --> 00:02:48.678
a new seed; the old one is burned.

00:02:49.978 --> 00:02:52.493
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:02:52.493 --> 00:02:55.638
Next up, Lesson 12.0: Mastery Starter.
